[59.507275, "o", "   180\t| `--format` | string | `\"json\"` | Output format: `\"json\"` or `\"dot\"`. |\r\n   181\t\r\n   182\t**JSON output schema:**\r\n   183\t```json\r\n   184\t{\r\n   185\t  \"nodes\": [\"<step_id>\", ...],\r\n   186\t  \"edges\": [[\"<from>\", \"<to>\"], ...],\r\n   187\t  \"topological_order\": [\"<step_id>\", ...],\r\n   188\t  \"parallel_groups\": [[\"<step_id>\", ...], ...],\r\n   189\t"]
[59.507384, "o", "  \"critical_path\": [\"<step_id>\", ...],\r\n   190\t  \"transitive_deps\": { \"<step_id>\": [\"<dep_id>\", ...], ... }\r\n   191\t}\r\n   192\t```\r\n   193\t\r\n   194\t- `nodes`: All step IDs, sorted alphabetically.\r\n   195\t- `edges`: Directed edges `[dependency, dependent]` (i.e., `[A, B]` means A must complete before B). Sorted lexicographically by `[from, to]`.\r\n   196\t- `topological_order`: Steps in a valid execution order. When multiple orderings are possible, use **alphabetical tie-breaking** among steps whose dependencies are all satisfied.\r\n   197\t- `parallel_groups`: Groups of steps that can execute concurrently. Each group contains steps whose dependencies are all in previous groups. Steps within each group are sorted alphabetically. Groups are ordered by execution phase.\r\n   198\t- `critical_path`: The longest path through the dependency graph (by number of steps). If there are ties, choose the lexicographically smallest path (compare element by element).\r\n   199\t- `transitive_deps`: For each step, the full set of trans"]
[59.507468, "o", "itive dependencies (not just direct). Each list sorted alphabetically. Steps with no dependencies have an empty list.\r\n   200\t\r\n   201\t**DOT output:** Standard Graphviz DOT format:\r\n   202\t```dot\r\n   203\tdigraph \"pipeline-name\" {\r\n   204\t  \"step-a\";\r\n   205\t  \"step-b\";\r\n   206\t  \"step-c\";\r\n   207\t  \"step-a\" -> \"step-b\";\r\n   208\t  \"step-a\" -> \"step-c\";\r\n   209\t}\r\n   210\t```\r\n   211\tNodes sorted alphabetically. Edges sorted lexicographically by `[from, to]`.\r\n   212\t\r\n   213\t**Exit codes:**\r\n   214\t| Code | Meaning |\r\n   215\t|------|---------|\r\n   216\t| `0` | Success. |\r\n   217\t"]
[59.507534, "o", "| `2` | Fatal error (invalid manifest, cycle detected). |\r\n   218\t\r\n   219\t---\r\n   220\t\r\n   221\t## 4. Dependency Graph Analysis\r\n   222\t\r\n   223\t### 4.1 Cycle Detection\r\n   224\t\r\n   225\tBefore any execution, the tool must validate that the dependency graph is acyclic. If a cycle is detected, the tool must exit with code 2 and print to stderr:\r\n   226\t\r\n   227\t```"]
[59.507629, "o", "\r\n   228\terror: cycle detected: step-a -> step-b -> step-c -> step-a\r\n   229\t```\r\n   230\t\r\n   231\tThe cycle must be reported as the **shortest cycle** found. If multiple shortest cycles exist, report the one that is lexicographically smallest (compare the cycle starting from its smallest element, then rotating to start from that element).\r\n   232\t\r\n   233\t### 4.2 Topological Sort\r\n   234\t\r\n   235\tSteps are executed in topological order with **alphabetical tie-breaking**: among all steps whose dependencies are fully satisfied, the one with the lexicographically smallest ID is executed first.\r\n   236\t\r\n"]
[59.507702, "o", "   237\t### 4.3 Parallel Groups\r\n   238\t\r\n   239\tParallel groups represent the minimum number of sequential phases needed to execute the pipeline. Each group contains all steps that *can* execute in that phase (all their dependencies are in earlier groups).\r\n   240\t\r\n   241\tAlgorithm:\r\n   242\t1. Group 0: all steps with no dependencies.\r\n   243\t2. Group N: all steps whose dependencies are all in groups 0 through N-1, and which are not already assigned to an earlier group. A step is assigned to the **earliest possible group**.\r\n   244\t3. Steps within each group are sorted alphabetically.\r\n   245\t\r\n   246\t### 4.4 Critical Path\r\n   247\t\r\n   248\tThe critical path is the longest path (by number of steps/nodes, not edges) through the DAG. Formally:\r\n   249\t1. For each node, compute the longest path ending at that node.\r\n"]
[59.507785, "o", "   250\t2. The critical path is the longest among all such paths.\r\n   251\t3. Ties are broken lexicographically: compare paths element-by-element, preferring the lexicographically smaller step ID at each position. Among paths of the same length, the one that is lexicographically smallest when compared as a sequence of strings wins.\r\n   252\t\r\n   253\t### 4.5 Transitive Dependencies\r\n   254\t\r\n   255\tFor each step, compute the full set of all steps it transitively depends on (not just direct dependencies). If step A depends on step B, and step B depends on step C, then step A's transitive dependencies include both B and C.\r\n   256\t\r\n   257\t---\r\n   258\t\r\n   259\t## 5. Fingerprinting\r\n   260\t\r\n   261\t### 5.1 Algorithms\r\n   262\t\r\n   263\t| Algorithm | Implementation | Digest Length |\r\n   264\t|-----------|---------------|---------------|\r\n   265\t| `sha256` | Go `crypto/sha256` | 32 bytes (64 hex chars) |\r\n"]
[59.507855, "o", "   266\t| `blake2b` | Go `crypto/sha512` — specifically, use `crypto/sha512` is wrong. Use `golang.org/x/crypto/blake2b` — **NO.** Since the tool uses only the standard library, use `crypto/sha256` for sha256 and for `blake2b`, implement BLAKE2b-256 using the Go standard library's `hash` interface with a custom implementation based on the BLAKE2b reference specification, OR simply use the `sha512.Sum512` as a fallback: compute SHA-512 and truncate to 32 bytes. **Clarification**: for the `blake2b` algorithm option, compute SHA-512 of the file content and take the first 32 bytes (64 hex characters). This is labeled as `blake2b` in the output for identification purposes, even though the underlying hash is truncated SHA-512. |\r\n   267\t\r\n   268\t### 5.2 Fingerprint Format\r\n   269\t\r\n   270\tAll fingerprints use the format:\r\n   271\t```\r\n   272\t<algorithm>:<hex_digest>\r\n   273\t```\r\n   274\t\r\n"]
[59.507965, "o", "   275\tWhere `hex_digest` is lowercase hexadecimal, always 64 characters.\r\n   276\t\r\n   277\t### 5.3 Fingerprinting Process\r\n   278\t\r\n   279\t1. Read the entire file content as raw bytes.\r\n   280\t2. Compute the hash using the selected algorithm.\r\n   281\t3. Format as `<algorithm>:<hex_digest>`.\r\n   282\t\r\n   283\tNo content normalization is applied during fingerprinting. Normalization (if any) is the responsibility of the step that produces the output.\r\n   284\t\r\n   285\t---\r\n   286\t\r\n"]
[59.508036, "o", "   287\t## 6. Multi-Pass Verification\r\n   288\t\r\n   289\t### 6.1 Execution Flow\r\n   290\t\r\n   291\tFor each verification pass `p` (from 1 to `verification.passes`):\r\n   292\t1. Create a fresh temporary directory as the pipeline root for this pass.\r\n   293\t2. Copy all input files from the original pipeline root into the temp directory.\r\n   294\t3. Execute all steps in topological order within the temp directory.\r\n   295\t4. After all steps complete, fingerprint all declared output files.\r\n   296\t5. Record the fingerprints for this pass.\r\n   297\t\r\n   298\t### 6.2 Cross-Pass Consistency\r\n   299\t\r\n   300\tAfter all passes complete, for each output file:\r\n"]
[59.508103, "o", "   301\t1. Collect fingerprints from all passes.\r\n   302\t2. If all fingerprints are identical, the output is **cross-pass consistent**.\r\n   303\t3. If any fingerprint differs, the output is **non-deterministic**.\r\n   304\t\r\n   305\t### 6.3 Reference Matching\r\n   306\t\r\n   307\tIf the manifest includes `references`:\r\n   308\t1. For each referenced output, compare the pass-1 fingerprint against the reference.\r\n   309\t2. If they match, the output **matches the reference**.\r\n   310\t3. If they don't match, the output has a **reference mismatch**.\r\n"]
[59.508171, "o", "   311\t\r\n   312\t### 6.4 Overall Verification Result\r\n   313\t\r\n   314\t- `\"PASS\"`: All outputs are cross-pass consistent AND all referenced outputs match their references.\r\n   315\t- `\"FAIL\"`: At least one output is non-deterministic or has a reference mismatch, but no execution errors occurred.\r\n   316\t- `\"ERROR\"`: At least one step failed to execute (non-zero exit code, timeout, or missing outputs).\r\n   317\t\r\n   318\t---\r\n   319\t\r\n   320\t## 7. Verification Report Schema\r\n"]
