[2764.656249, "o", "    12\t## 13. Verification Policy Engine\r\n    13\t\r\n    14\t### 13.1 Policy Section in Manifest\r\n    15\t\r\n"]
[2764.656465, "o", "    16\tThe manifest gains an optional `policy` section:\r\n    17\t\r\n    18\t```json\r\n    19\t{\r\n    20\t  \"policy\": {\r\n    21\t    \"default_action\": \"strict\",\r\n    22\t    \"rules\": [\r\n    23\t      {\r\n    24\t        \"id\": \"<string>\",\r\n    25\t        \"type\": \"<string>\",\r\n    26\t        \"params\": { ... },\r\n    27\t        \"action\": \"allow\"\r\n    28\t      }\r\n    29\t    ]\r\n    30\t  }\r\n    31\t}\r\n    32\t```\r\n    33\t\r\n    34\t| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |\r\n    35\t|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|\r\n    36\t| `policy` | object | no | — | If absent, strict verification applies (identical to v1 behavior). |\r\n"]
[2764.656675, "o", "    37\t| `policy.default_action` | string | no | `\"strict\"` | `\"strict\"`: all outputs must match. `\"lenient\"`: treat non-determinism as warnings unless a rule specifically requires a match. |\r\n    38\t| `policy.rules` | array | yes (if policy present) | — | Ordered list of policy rules. Rules are evaluated in order; the **first matching rule** for a given step/output determines the action. |\r\n    39\t\r\n"]
[2764.657388, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@n30009:/app# "]
[2774.835808, "i", "nl -ba /app/docs/spec_v2.md | sed -n '40,110p'\n"]
[2774.836127, "o", "nl -ba /app/docs/spec_v2.md | sed -n '40,110p'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[2774.839637, "o", "    40\t### 13.2 Rule Schema\r\n    41\t\r\n    42\t```json\r\n    43\t{\r\n    44\t  \"id\": \"<string>\",\r\n    45\t  \"type\": \"<string>\",\r\n    46\t  \"params\": { ... },\r\n"]
[2774.839755, "o", "    47\t  \"action\": \"<string>\"\r\n    48\t}\r\n    49\t```\r\n    50\t\r\n    51\t| Field | Type | Required | Description |\r\n    52\t|-------|------|----------|-------------|\r\n    53\t| `id` | string | yes | Unique rule identifier. Must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$`. |\r\n    54\t| `type` | string | yes | Rule type (see below). |\r\n    55\t| `params` | object | yes | Type-specific parameters. |\r\n    56\t| `action` | string | yes | `\"allow\"`, `\"warn\"`, or `\"require\"`. |\r\n    57\t\r\n    58\t### 13.3 Rule Types\r\n    59\t\r\n    60\t#### `allow_variance`\r\n    61\t\r\n    62\tAllows specific outputs to be non-deterministic without failing verification.\r\n    63\t\r\n"]
[2774.839846, "o", "    64\t**Params:**\r\n    65\t```json\r\n    66\t{\r\n    67\t  \"output_pattern\": \"<glob pattern>\"\r\n    68\t}\r\n    69\t```\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t`output_pattern` is a glob pattern matched against output file paths (e.g., `\"*.log\"`, `\"tmp/*\"`). Uses `filepath.Match` semantics.\r\n    72\t\r\n    73\t**Behavior:** For outputs matching the pattern: if the output is non-deterministic (different across passes) or has a reference mismatch, and the rule's action is `\"allow\"`, the step's effective status changes from `FAIL` to `PASS` (for that output). If action is `\"warn\"`, the status stays `FAIL` but a warning is emitted. If action is `\"require\"`, the output **must** be non-deterministic (if it's actually consistent, the rule marks it as a policy violation).\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\t#### `min_consistency_rate`\r\n    76\t\r\n    77\tRequires that at least a specified fraction of passes produce identical output, but does not require 100% consistency.\r\n"]
[2774.839919, "o", "    78\t\r\n    79\t**Params:**\r\n    80\t```json\r\n    81\t{\r\n    82\t  \"rate\": <float>,\r\n    83\t  \"step_pattern\": \"<glob pattern>\"\r\n    84\t}\r\n    85\t```\r\n    86"]
[2774.84006, "o", "\t\r\n    87\t- `rate`: A float between 0.0 and 1.0. For example, `0.75` means at least 75% of passes must agree.\r\n    88\t- `step_pattern`: Glob pattern matching step IDs. Use `\"*\"` for all steps.\r\n    89\t\r\n    90\t**Behavior:** For each output of matching steps, compute the **majority rate**: the fraction of passes that produced the most common fingerprint. If `majority_rate >= rate` and action is `\"allow\"`, treat the output as effectively consistent. If `majority_rate < rate` and action is `\"require\"`, it's a policy violation.\r\n    91\t\r\n    92\t**Majority rate computation:** Given N passes and fingerprints f1..fN, count occurrences of each unique fingerprint. The majority rate is `max_count / N`. For single-pass verification, majority rate is always 1.0.\r\n    93\t\r\n    94\t#### `required_steps`\r\n    95\t\r\n    96\tSpecifies that only certain steps are required to pass; failures in other steps don't affect the overall policy status.\r\n    97\t\r\n    98\t**Params:**\r\n    99\t```json\r\n   100\t{\r\n   101\t  \"steps\": [\"<step_id>\","]
[2774.840131, "o", " ...]\r\n   102\t}\r\n   103\t```\r\n   104\t\r\n   105\t**Behavior:** When evaluating policy_overall_status:\r\n   106\t- If action is `\"require\"`: all listed steps must have status PASS (after other policy rules). If any listed step is FAIL/ERROR/TIMEOUT, overall policy status is FAIL/ERROR.\r\n   107\t- If action is `\"allow\"`: unlisted steps that are FAIL/ERROR are forgiven — they don't affect policy_overall_status. Listed steps still follow normal rules.\r\n   108\t\r\n   109\t#### `ignore_steps`\r\n   110\t\r\n"]
